Patch 2051: an uploaded vertex-program word is compared as one 32-bit value, not with a memcmp call.

Titles upload a draw's vertex program before every draw (NV4097_SET_TRANSFORM_PROGRAM), mostly the one already there,
and each word was checked with a 4-byte memcmp, which this build does not inline: memcmp was about 6% of the drawing
thread in J-Stars' demo fights (no caller name in the sampled stacks). Same result, one load and one compare.

Tested (US disc, bench.ps1): demo 21.60 / 21.96 / 21.62 flips/s (median 21.62, before 20.68), drain per frame
20.5 ms (before 22.9), Free Battle 21.77 (before 20.00); pictures right (bench\exp\2026-11-07_1842-vpword).

diff --git a/libs/video/rsx_commands.c b/libs/video/rsx_commands.c
index 3b5b8673..d5eb4f44 100644
--- a/libs/video/rsx_commands.c
+++ b/libs/video/rsx_commands.c
@@ -353,9 +353,11 @@ static inline void rsx_vp_ucode_word(rsx_state* state, u32 data)
         /* Titles upload a draw's program before every draw, mostly the
          * one already there. Only a changed word changes the program
          * (vp_dirty lets the backend skip looking it up again). */
-        const u8 b[4] = { (u8)data, (u8)(data >> 8), (u8)(data >> 16), (u8)(data >> 24) };
-        if (memcmp(&state->vp_ucode[w], b, 4) != 0) {
-            memcpy(&state->vp_ucode[w], b, 4);
+        /* One 32-bit compare, not a memcmp call: this runs for every word of every upload (patch 2051; the
+         * memcmp calls were ~6% of the drawing thread in J-Stars' fights). Little-endian host, as before. */
+        u32 cur; memcpy(&cur, &state->vp_ucode[w], 4);
+        if (cur != data) {
+            memcpy(&state->vp_ucode[w], &data, 4);
             state->vp_dirty = 1;
         }
         state->vp_ucode_write = w + 4;
